1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,845
Your disk is almost full, and the application cannot stop.

2
00:00:03,865 --> 00:00:06,712
You do not copy anything, and you do not reboot.

3
00:00:06,732 --> 00:00:10,535
You just make the volume bigger, while it is mounted and in use.

4
00:00:11,005 --> 00:00:14,334
In this video: extend existing logical volumes.

5
00:00:18,854 --> 00:00:21,437
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:21,907 --> 00:00:24,169
Extend existing logical volumes.

7
00:00:24,639 --> 00:00:27,490
We'll grow a logical volume and its file system,

8
00:00:27,510 --> 00:00:32,513
hit the case where the volume group has no room left, and fix that by adding a disk.

9
00:00:41,033 --> 00:00:43,577
A logical volume lives in a volume group,

10
00:00:43,597 --> 00:00:46,828
and the group draws its space from physical volumes.

11
00:00:46,848 --> 00:00:50,080
To make a volume bigger, the group needs free space.

12
00:00:54,550 --> 00:01:00,058
There are two things to grow: the logical volume, and the file system inside it.

13
00:01:00,078 --> 00:01:04,343
Grow only the volume, and the file system stays the same size.

14
00:01:04,363 --> 00:01:07,522
Lvextend with dash r does both in one command.

15
00:01:12,992 --> 00:01:17,060
If the group has no free space, you first add a disk to it,

16
00:01:17,080 --> 00:01:19,208
with P V create and V G extend.

17
00:01:19,228 --> 00:01:24,751
Before the demo, silently, the lab builds a one gigabyte X F S volume mounted on

18
00:01:24,771 --> 00:01:28,493
slash M N T slash app, with a blank second disk ready.

19
00:01:28,513 --> 00:01:30,572
These steps change the system.

20
00:01:41,092 --> 00:01:45,246
First, look at the group. Vgs shows the group and its free space:

21
00:01:45,266 --> 00:01:47,237
just under four gigabytes free.

22
00:01:51,707 --> 00:01:54,383
D F with dash h shows the file system:

23
00:01:54,403 --> 00:01:58,143
about one gigabyte, mounted on slash M N T slash app.

24
00:01:58,163 --> 00:02:00,413
This is the volume we will grow.

25
00:02:06,933 --> 00:02:11,892
Lvextend, dash capital L, plus five twelve M, and the path of the volume.

26
00:02:11,912 --> 00:02:16,257
The plus matters: it means add this much, not make it this size.

27
00:02:20,727 --> 00:02:23,952
The volume went from one gigabyte to one and a half.

28
00:02:23,972 --> 00:02:28,195
But D F still shows the old size. The file system has not grown yet.

29
00:02:34,715 --> 00:02:40,223
For X F S, the grow command is X F S underscore grow F S, with the mount point.

30
00:02:40,243 --> 00:02:45,191
It prints a long report, so we show only the first line and the result.

31
00:02:51,661 --> 00:02:57,368
Now D F shows one point five gigabytes, and the volume was extended while mounted.

32
00:02:57,388 --> 00:03:01,070
For ext four, the matching command is resize two F S.

33
00:03:07,590 --> 00:03:12,701
Now we want six gigabytes more, with dash r, which also grows the file system.

34
00:03:12,721 --> 00:03:15,662
It fails. We need fifteen thirty six extents,

35
00:03:15,682 --> 00:03:20,135
and only eight ninety five are available. The group is out of space.

36
00:03:25,605 --> 00:03:27,462
So we add the second disk.

37
00:03:27,482 --> 00:03:31,793
P V create marks slash dev slash V D C as a physical volume.

38
00:03:35,263 --> 00:03:38,806
V G extend adds it to the group. Now the group has room.

39
00:03:45,326 --> 00:03:49,530
We run the same lvextend command again. This time it works.

40
00:03:49,550 --> 00:03:55,472
It finds X F S, grows the volume from one and a half to seven and a half gigabytes,

41
00:03:55,492 --> 00:04:00,197
and grows the file system too. Dash r saved us the second command.

42
00:04:07,717 --> 00:04:12,402
D F shows seven and a half gigabytes, and cat shows the data is untouched.

43
00:04:12,422 --> 00:04:14,182
We never unmounted anything.

44
00:04:20,702 --> 00:04:24,138
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

45
00:04:25,658 --> 00:04:31,394
We reboot. L V S shows seven and a half gigabytes, D F agrees, and the data is there.

46
00:04:31,414 --> 00:04:37,286
The change is permanent, because it lives in the L V M metadata and in the file system.

47
00:04:48,806 --> 00:04:52,799
Your turn. The volume group vgexam has one small disk,

48
00:04:52,819 --> 00:04:56,886
and the logical volume lvexam is six hundred megabytes,

49
00:04:56,906 --> 00:04:59,413
mounted on slash M N T slash exam.

50
00:04:59,433 --> 00:05:03,426
Add the blank disk slash dev slash V D C to the group,

51
00:05:03,446 --> 00:05:08,925
and extend lvexam to two gigabytes, file system included. Run the checker.

52
00:05:16,395 --> 00:05:18,145
Try before you open the solution.

53
00:05:23,665 --> 00:05:27,080
Next up: diagnose and correct file permission problems.

54
00:05:27,550 --> 00:05:30,452
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
